```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A subset $A$ of an abelian group $G$ is said to be \emph{complete} if every element of $G$ can be expressed as a sum of distinct elements of $A$. A central problem in additive combinatorics is to determine the minimum cardinality of a complete subset in the cyclic group $\mathbb{Z}_n$. For the case where $n=p$ is a prime, Olson resolved a key problem related to the Erd\H{o}s--Heilbronn conjecture by establishing an optimal lower bound on the size of sub-sum sets, implying that $A \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$ is complete once $|A| \gg \sqrt{p}$. For general $n$, Vu subsequently proved that there exists an absolute constant $c$ such that any subset $A \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_n$ satisfying $|A| > c\sqrt{n}$ is complete. In this paper, we refine these results by establishing an explicit constant for general $n$. Specifically, we prove that any subset $A \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_n$ is complete provided that
\[
|A| > 1 + 2\sqrt{n-4}.
\]
\end{abstract}

\end{document}
```

